-    "content": "# [Guide] Scripted Workflows and Transition Helpers\n\n## Overview\n\nIn traditional project management tools, workflow rules are either simple notification triggers or inflexible webhooks that fire only *after* a change has occurred. If an issue is improperly moved to \"Done\" without meeting code coverage requirements or QA verification, the damage is already done.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban implements a **Scripted Workflow Engine** with **Pre-Transition Helpers**. When an operator or agent attempts to move a work item between states (`from_state -> to_state`), the transition is evaluated through an ordered pipeline of synchronous validators, field mutators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n---\n\n## The Transition Helper Pipeline\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Drag[\"User / Agent moves card: In Progress -> Done\"] --> HelperEngine[\"Transition Helper Engine\"]\n    HelperEngine --> Stage1[\"Stage 1: Synchronous Validator (Tier A Wasm)<br/>- Verify blockers resolved<br/>- Verify Definition of Done criteria & PR links\"]\n    Stage1 -->|PASS| Stage2[\"Stage 2: Synchronous Field Mutator (Tier A)<br/>- Auto-set closed_at timestamp<br/>- Update cycle velocity counters\"]\n    Stage1 -->|VETO| VetoInstant[\"Transition Rejected (422 Unprocessable)\"]\n    Stage2 --> Stage3[\"Stage 3: Asynchronous Gate Hold (Tier B Async Agent)<br/>- Card enters pending_agent_gate hold (\u23f3\ud83e\udd16)<br/>- Dispatches forensic audit to agent\"]\n    Stage3 -->|Approved| Approved[\"Gate Approved (\u2705\ud83e\udd16)<br/>Card lands cleanly in Done column\"]\n    Stage3 -->|Vetoed| VetoSnap[\"Gate Veto (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16)<br/>240ms spring snap-back to In Progress<br/>Itemized rejection posted to activity feed\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Helper Architecture\n\nTransition helpers operate across two execution tiers to balance latency and verification depth:\n\n1. **Tier A (Synchronous Wasm / In-Process):** Fast sub-25ms validators and mutators executing in memory or within sandboxed WebAssembly runtimes. These guarantee instantaneous rejection or field stamping without network round-trips.\n2. **Tier B (Asynchronous Autonomous Agent Gates):** Non-blocking background gates dispatched to local CLI harnesses (`agy -p`, `codex -p`), in-app SDK runners, or remote bare Git mirrors. These run comprehensive multi-file audits, build verifications, and forensic QA checks before approving the transition.\n\n### Failure Policies\n\nEvery attached transition helper specifies an explicit failure policy to determine behavior when a helper times out, errors, or fails execution:\n\n- **Fail-Closed:** Blocks the transition immediately. The card snaps back to its origin column and an itemized failure explanation is appended to the ticket timeline.\n- **Fail-Open (Warn):** Logs an audit warning to the activity feed and ledger while allowing the transition to proceed. This is reserved for non-critical informational checks.\n\n---\n\n## Admin Workflow Transition Studio\n\nProject Administrators configure transition pipelines through the visual studio at `/projects/KAN/settings/workflows`.\n\n![Workflow Transition Studio and Dry-Run Drawer](images/15-workflow-transition-studio.png)\n\n### Studio Capabilities\n- **State Transition Matrix:** Select any valid transition path (e.g. `In Progress -> In Review`, `In Review -> Done`).\n- **Pipeline Reordering:** Drag to reorder validators and mutators in the execution chain.\n- **Mutable Field Checkboxes:** Explicitly define which fields the transition helper is permitted to modify, enforcing least-privilege mutation boundaries.\n\n### The Non-Persistent Dry-Run Drawer\nTesting workflow logic against live tickets risks corrupting sprint metrics. The Workflow Studio provides a **Zero-Persistence Dry-Run Drawer**:\n- Select an existing ticket fixture or pick a live issue.\n- Execute the transition pipeline in a simulated sandbox without mutating the database.\n- Inspect per-step execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms` Wasm validator, `3.4ms` security invariant validator, `2.1ms` auto-label mutator).\n- Preview proposed property mutations (such as automatically applied labels).\n- Copy the exact CLI validation command (`kanban-agent validate-transition`) for local offline debugging.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Workflow Architect\n    participant Studio as Workflow Studio\n    participant Drawer as Dry-Run Drawer\n    participant Engine as Helper Engine (Sandbox)\n\n    Admin->>Studio: Select \"In Review -> Done\" rule\n    Admin->>Studio: Click \"Dry-Run Against Sample Ticket\"\n    Studio->>Drawer: Open Dry-Run Drawer with sample payload\n    Admin->>Drawer: Click \"Run Dry-Run\"\n    Drawer->>Engine: Dispatch pipeline in dry_run=True mode\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 1: qa-proof-validator (1.2ms, Allowed)\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 2: security-invariant-validator (3.4ms, Allowed)\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 3: auto-label-mutator (2.1ms, Applied labels)\n    Engine-->>Drawer: Return execution report & copyable CLI snippet\n    Drawer-->>Admin: Display green \"Transition Allowed: Changes would commit\"\n```\n\n![Workflow Studio Dry-Run Drawer Simulation](images/50-workflow-studio-dry-run-drawer.png)\n\n---\n\n## Read-Only Bare Git Repository Bindings\n\nAutonomous agents evaluating transition gates often require access to source code, diff trees, and commit histories. To satisfy this requirement securely without risking repository corruption:\n- Administrators can bind local bare Git mirrors directly to a project through the **Git Repository Bindings** dialog.\n- The volume mount is strictly enforced as read-only (`:ro bare mirror`).\n- Agents can run `git diff`, `git log`, and code analysis against the mirror, but all write attempts fail at the OS kernel boundary.\n\n![Git Repository Bindings Modal](images/51-workflow-bind-git-repo-modal.png)\n\n---\n\n## Asynchronous Agent Gate Lifecycle on the Board\n\nWhen an issue transition requires complex verification (such as forensic QA certification or automated security audits), the transition operates asynchronously without blocking the user interface.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> InProgress\n    InProgress --> PendingGate: Move to Done (Trigger Transition Helper)\n    PendingGate --> Done: Agent Certifies Criteria (\u2705\ud83e\udd16 Approved)\n    PendingGate --> InProgress: Agent Rejects (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16 240ms Snap-Back)\n    Done --> [*]\n```\n\n![Async Agent Gate Card States and Snap-Back Lifecycle](images/16-async-agent-gate-states.png)\n\n![Board Pending Gate State in Active Sprint](images/35-board-pending-gate.png)\n\n### The Board Interaction States\n\n1. **Pending Hold (`\u23f3\ud83e\udd16`):**\n   - The card displays an amber countdown timer and progress indicator.\n   - The ticket enters database status `pending_agent_gate`.\n   - Other users viewing the board see the real-time hold status over WebSockets.\n2. **Gate Veto and 240ms Spring Snap-Back (`\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16`):**\n   - If the agent discovers a defect or missing verification proof, the gate is vetoed.\n   - The board executes a physics-based 240ms spring snap-back animation, returning the card to its original column.\n   - A non-intrusive toast notification alerts the user, and the agent automatically posts an itemized rejection explanation to the ticket's `\ud83e\udd16 Gates` activity feed.\n3. **Gate Approval (`\u2705\ud83e\udd16`):**\n   - When all criteria are certified, the gate resolves to approved.\n   - The card transitions smoothly into the destination column, stamped with an immutable cryptographic verification badge.\n\n---\n\n## The Easiest 3-Step Recipe to Implement an Automated QA Gate on `Done`\n\nYou can enforce automated Definition-of-Done verification on any project board in three steps without writing custom glue code:\n\n### Step 1: Register and Probe Your AI Provider (`/admin/ai`)\n1. Open **AI Providers & Token Caps Studio** at `/admin/ai` and click **`+ Register Provider`**.\n2. Place your API secret key in the **API Secret Key** field at the top of the drawer (optional for local Ollama), configure the **HTTP Base URL** and hard **Cutoff Timeout (ms)** (for example `20000` ms or `35000` ms), and click **`\ud83d\udd0d Probe Models`** to replace defaults exclusively with live discovered models.\n\n![Gemini Provider Editor with Top API Key and Probed Models](images/62-ai-provider-gemini-top-key-probe.png)\n\n![Ollama Provider Editor with Live Probed Models and Cutoff Timeout](images/63-ai-provider-ollama-probed-models.png)\n\n### Step 2: Register an Agent Definition (`/admin/agents`)\n1. Open **Autonomous Agent Definitions Studio** at `/admin/agents` and click **`+ Register Agent`**.\n2. Choose **In-App SDK**, pick your provider (`ollama`, `openai`, or `gemini`), click one of the **Probed Models** quick-pick chips (such as `gemma3:4b` or `gpt-4o-mini`), set the timeout cutoff, and enter your QA rubric in **System Instructions**.\n\n![QA Done Gate Agent Definition with Probed Model Quick-Picks](images/64-agent-qa-done-definition.png)\n\n### Step 3: Attach the Hook to `In Progress -> Done` via `+ Workflow`\n1. On the Kanban board, click **`+ Workflow`** on the **`Done`** column header (or open `/projects/KAN/settings/workflows`).\n2. Select `From State: In Progress`, `To State: Done`, `Hook Kind: Agent Transition Helper`, your `@qa-done-gate` agent, and `Failure Policy: Fail-Closed`, then click **Attach Hook**.\n\n![Attach Workflow Helper Hook Dialog for In Progress to Done](images/65-workflow-attach-qa-done-gate.png)\n\n---\n\n## In-Transition Gate Ideas Gallery\n\n### Idea 1: Fast 2B/4B Gemma Triage & Auto-Labeler (`Backlog -> Todo`)\nAttach a fast local edge model (`gemma4:e2b` or `granite3.3:2b`, `timeout_ms: 15000`) with `allowed_mutable_fields: [\"labels\", \"priority\"]` to automatically classify incoming backlog tickets, apply `needs-triage` or component labels, and reject empty one-line placeholders before sprint planning.\n\n### Idea 2: Security & Secret Scan Gate (`Todo -> In Progress`)\nAttach an `agent_interceptor` or Tier A Wasm `sync_validator` on `Todo -> In Progress` to verify that work items touching authentication, RBAC, or database migrations include a threat model checklist and zero pasted credentials before engineering begins.\n\n### Idea 3: Automated Definition-of-Done QA Gate (`In Progress -> Done`)\nRequire completed acceptance criteria and documented unit/QA verification evidence before any card can land in `Done`:\n- **Positive Transition (`KAN-1` Approved):** A ticket with checked Acceptance Criteria and documented Verification Proof passes the gate, lands in `Done`, and receives a persisted `## \u2705\ud83e\udd16 Workflow Transition Gate Approved` rationale comment.\n\n![Positive Gate Approval Landing Ticket in Done](images/66-qa-gate-positive-approved-done.png)\n\n- **Negative Transition (`KAN-2` Vetoed):** An unverified or impossible ticket (such as `\"Build a rocket ship and serve ice cream on Saturn\"` with no design doc, no tests, and no proof) is **vetoed** and kept safely in `In Progress` with an itemized `\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16 Gate` rejection comment.\n\n![Negative Gate Veto Blocking Saturn Ice Cream Ticket in In Progress](images/67-qa-gate-negative-saturn-ice-cream-vetoed.png)\n\n"
+    "content": "# [Guide] Scripted Workflows and Transition Helpers\n\n## Overview\n\nIn traditional project management tools, workflow rules are either simple notification triggers or inflexible webhooks that fire only *after* a change has occurred. If an issue is improperly moved to \"Done\" without meeting code coverage requirements or QA verification, the damage is already done.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban implements a **Scripted Workflow Engine** with **Pre-Transition Helpers**. When an operator or agent attempts to move a work item between states (`from_state -> to_state`), the transition is evaluated through an ordered pipeline of synchronous validators, field mutators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n---\n\n## The Transition Helper Pipeline\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Drag[\"User / Agent moves card: In Progress -> Done\"] --> HelperEngine[\"Transition Helper Engine\"]\n    HelperEngine --> Stage1[\"Stage 1: Synchronous Validator (Tier A Wasm) - Verify blockers resolved - Verify Definition of Done criteria & PR links\"]\n    Stage1 -->|PASS| Stage2[\"Stage 2: Synchronous Field Mutator (Tier A) - Auto-tag labels & priority - Update cycle velocity counters\"]\n    Stage1 -->|VETO| VetoInstant[\"Transition Rejected (422 Unprocessable)\"]\n    Stage2 --> Stage3[\"Stage 3: Asynchronous Gate Hold (Tier B Async Agent) - Card enters pending_agent_gate hold (\u23f3\ud83e\udd16) - Dispatches forensic audit to agent\"]\n    Stage3 -->|Approved| Approved[\"Gate Approved (\u2705\ud83e\udd16) - Card lands cleanly in Done column\"]\n    Stage3 -->|Vetoed| VetoSnap[\"Gate Veto (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16) - 240ms spring snap-back to In Progress - Itemized rejection posted to activity feed\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Helper Architecture\n\nTransition helpers operate across two execution tiers to balance latency and verification depth:\n\n1. **Tier A (Synchronous Wasm / In-Process):** Fast sub-25ms validators and mutators executing in memory or within sandboxed WebAssembly runtimes. These guarantee instantaneous rejection or field stamping without network round-trips.\n2. **Tier B (Asynchronous Autonomous Agent Gates):** Non-blocking background gates dispatched to local CLI harnesses (`agy -p`, `codex -p`), in-app SDK runners, or remote bare Git mirrors. These run comprehensive multi-file audits, build verifications, and forensic QA checks before approving the transition.\n\n### Failure Policies\n\nEvery attached transition helper specifies an explicit failure policy to determine behavior when a helper times out, errors, or fails execution:\n\n- **Fail-Closed:** Blocks the transition immediately. The card snaps back to its origin column and an itemized failure explanation is appended to the ticket timeline.\n- **Fail-Open (Warn):** Logs an audit warning to the activity feed and ledger while allowing the transition to proceed. This is reserved for non-critical informational checks.\n\n---\n\n## Admin Workflow Transition Studio\n\nProject Administrators configure transition pipelines through the visual studio at `/projects/KAN/settings/workflows`.\n\n![Workflow Transition Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice5-workflow-studio-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Studio Capabilities\n- **State Transition Matrix:** Select any valid transition path (e.g. `In Progress -> In Review`, `In Review -> Done`).\n- **Pipeline Reordering:** Drag to reorder validators and mutators in the execution chain.\n- **Mutable Field Checkboxes:** Explicitly define which fields the transition helper is permitted to modify, enforcing least-privilege mutation boundaries.\n\n![Attach Workflow Hook Dialog](images/slice5-workflow-attach-hook-dialog-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Attaching Workflow Transition Hooks with Dry-Run\n\n1. **Open Workflow Studio:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows`.\n2. **Select Transition Rule:** Locate the transition rule card matching your target lifecycle change (e.g. `In Progress -> Done` or `In Review -> Done`).\n3. **Click Attach Hook:** Click the **+ Attach Hook** button on the transition rule card to open the Attach Workflow Hook Dialog.\n4. **Configure Hook Metadata:** Specify a descriptive Hook Name (e.g. `reality-checker-done-gate`), select the Hook Kind (`sync_validator`, `sync_mutator`, or `agent_interceptor`), and choose the Failure Policy (`fail_closed` to block transition on error/timeout, or `fail_open_warn` to log a warning while permitting the transition).\n5. **Select Engine or Agent Definition:** For Wasm modules, choose an approved module. For Agent Interceptors, select the registered Agent Definition (grouped by harness: CLI Pipe, In-App SDK, or Remote Runner); the dialog auto-populates the agent's real timeout (between 1 and 300,000 ms, e.g. `60000ms`). If using mutators, select permitted mutable fields (`labels`, `priority`, `assignees`, `start_date`, `due_date`, `cycle`, or `custom_fields.<slug>`).\n6. **Save Hook:** Click **Attach Hook** to save the rule to the pipeline.\n7. **Simulate in Dry-Run Drawer:** Click the **Dry-Run** button on the transition rule, select a sample ticket fixture or live issue key, click **Run Dry-Run** to execute the pipeline inside a simulated non-mutating sandbox, and inspect individual hook execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms`, `3.4ms`), returned logs, and allowed/vetoed verdicts before deploying to production.\n\n### The Non-Persistent Dry-Run Drawer\nTesting workflow logic against live tickets risks corrupting sprint metrics. The Workflow Studio provides a **Zero-Persistence Dry-Run Drawer**:\n- Select an existing ticket fixture or pick a live issue.\n- Execute the transition pipeline in a simulated sandbox without mutating the database.\n- Inspect per-step execution timings (e.g. `1.2ms` Wasm validator, `3.4ms` security invariant validator, `2.1ms` auto-label mutator).\n- Preview proposed property mutations (such as automatically applied labels).\n- Copy the exact CLI validation command (`kanban-agent validate-transition`) for local offline debugging.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Workflow Architect\n    participant Studio as Workflow Studio\n    participant Drawer as Dry-Run Drawer\n    participant Engine as Helper Engine (Sandbox)\n\n    Admin->>Studio: Select \"In Review -> Done\" rule\n    Admin->>Studio: Click \"Dry-Run Against Sample Ticket\"\n    Studio->>Drawer: Open Dry-Run Drawer with sample payload\n    Admin->>Drawer: Click \"Run Dry-Run\"\n    Drawer->>Engine: Dispatch pipeline in dry_run=True mode\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 1: qa-proof-validator (1.2ms, Allowed)\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 2: security-invariant-validator (3.4ms, Allowed)\n    Engine->>Engine: Run Hook 3: auto-label-mutator (2.1ms, Applied labels)\n    Engine-->>Drawer: Return execution report & copyable CLI snippet\n    Drawer-->>Admin: Display green \"Transition Allowed: Changes would commit\"\n```\n\n![Workflow Studio Dry-Run Drawer Simulation](images/slice5-workflow-dry-run-drawer-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n\n---\n\n## Read-Only Bare Git Repository Bindings\n\nAutonomous agents evaluating transition gates often require access to source code, diff trees, and commit histories. To satisfy this requirement securely without risking repository corruption:\n- Administrators can bind local bare Git mirrors directly to a project through the **Git Repository Bindings** dialog.\n- The volume mount is strictly enforced as read-only (`:ro bare mirror`).\n- Agents can run `git diff`, `git log`, and code analysis against the mirror, but all write attempts fail at the OS kernel boundary.\n\n![Git Repository Bindings Modal](images/slice5-workflow-git-repo-bindings-desktop-1440x900-light.png)\n![Git Repository Bindings Modal Architecture](images/51-workflow-bind-git-repo-modal.png)\n\n### Step-by-Step Guide: Binding Git Repositories\n\n1. **Access Project Repository Bindings:** Navigate to `/projects/<PROJECT_KEY>/settings/workflows` and scroll down to the Git Repository Bindings section.\n2. **Open Repository Binding Dialog:** Click **+ Bind Git Repository** to open the repository configuration drawer.\n3. **Specify Binding Parameters:** Enter the Repository Slug (e.g. `kanban-core`), remote repository URL (`remote_url`), default tracking branch or ref (`default_ref`, e.g. `main`), access mode (`ro_vfs_mount` or `bare_git_tools`), and optional credential binding (`workspace-pat` or `ssh-key`).\n4. **Enforce Read-Only Security Invariant:** The backend provisions a bare mirror under `/var/lib/kanban/repos/<project>/<slug>.git` with strict read-only POSIX permissions (`:ro`). Even if an agent gains shell execution capability inside a runner, commits or branch writes are rejected at the filesystem level.\n5. **Verify Live Mirror Refresh:** Click **Refresh Mirror** to trigger an immediate background fetch and inspect the last sync timestamp, head commit SHA, and branch count directly in the status table.\n\n---\n\n## Asynchronous Agent Gate Lifecycle on the Board\n\nWhen an issue transition requires complex verification (such as forensic QA certification or automated security audits), the transition operates asynchronously without blocking the user interface.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> InProgress\n    InProgress --> PendingGate: Move to Done (Trigger Transition Helper)\n    PendingGate --> Done: Agent Certifies Criteria (\u2705\ud83e\udd16 Approved)\n    PendingGate --> InProgress: Agent Rejects (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16 240ms Snap-Back)\n    Done --> [*]\n```\n\n![Async Agent Gate Card States and Snap-Back Lifecycle](images/16-async-agent-gate-states.png)\n\n![Board Pending Gate State in Active Sprint](images/35-board-pending-gate.png)\n\n### The Board Interaction States\n\n- **Pending Hold (\u23f3\ud83e\udd16):** The card displays an amber countdown timer and progress indicator. The ticket enters database status `pending_agent_gate`. Other users viewing the board see the real-time hold status over WebSockets.\n- **Gate Veto and 240ms Spring Snap-Back (\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16):** If the agent discovers a defect or missing verification proof, the gate is vetoed. The board executes a physics-based 240ms spring snap-back animation, returning the card to its original column. A non-intrusive toast notification alerts the user, and the agent automatically posts an itemized rejection explanation to the ticket's `\ud83e\udd16 Gates` activity feed.\n- **Gate Approval (\u2705\ud83e\udd16):** When all criteria are certified, the gate resolves to approved. The card transitions smoothly into the destination column, stamped with an immutable cryptographic verification badge.\n\n---\n\n## The Easiest 3-Step Recipe to Implement an Automated QA Gate on `Done`\n\nYou can enforce automated Definition-of-Done verification on any project board in three steps without writing custom glue code:\n\n### Step 1: Register and Probe Your AI Provider (`/admin/ai`)\n1. Open **AI Providers & Token Caps Studio** at `/admin/ai` and click **+ Register Provider**.\n2. Place your API secret key in the **API Secret Key** field at the top of the drawer (optional for local Ollama), configure the **HTTP Base URL** and hard **Cutoff Timeout (ms)** (for example `20000` ms or `35000` ms), and click **\ud83d\udd0d Probe Models** to replace defaults exclusively with live discovered models.\n\n![Gemini Provider Editor with Top API Key and Probed Models](images/62-ai-provider-gemini-top-key-probe.png)\n\n![Ollama Provider Editor with Live Probed Models and Cutoff Timeout](images/63-ai-provider-ollama-probed-models.png)\n\n### Step 2: Register an Agent Definition (`/admin/agents`)\n1. Open **Autonomous Agent Definitions Studio** at `/admin/agents` and click **+ Register Agent**.\n2. Choose **In-App SDK**, pick your provider (`ollama`, `openai`, or `gemini`), click one of the **Probed Models** quick-pick chips (such as `gemma3:4b` or `gpt-4o-mini`), set the timeout cutoff, and enter your QA rubric in **System Instructions**.\n\n![QA Done Gate Agent Definition with Probed Model Quick-Picks](images/64-agent-qa-done-definition.png)\n\n### Step 3: Attach the Hook to `In Progress -> Done` via `+ Workflow`\n1. On the Kanban board, click **+ Workflow** on the **Done** column header (or open `/projects/KAN/settings/workflows`).\n2. Select `From State: In Progress`, `To State: Done`, `Hook Kind: Agent Transition Helper`, your `@qa-done-gate` agent, and `Failure Policy: Fail-Closed`, then click **Attach Hook**.\n\n![Attach Workflow Helper Hook Dialog for In Progress to Done](images/65-workflow-attach-qa-done-gate.png)\n\n---\n\n## In-Transition Gate Ideas Gallery\n\n### Idea 1: Fast 2B/4B Gemma Triage & Auto-Labeler (`Backlog -> Todo`)\nAttach a fast local edge model (`gemma4:e2b` or `granite3.3:2b`, `timeout_ms: 15000`) with `allowed_mutable_fields: [\"labels\", \"priority\"]` to automatically classify incoming backlog tickets, apply `needs-triage` or component labels, and reject empty one-line placeholders before sprint planning.\n\n### Idea 2: Security & Secret Scan Gate (`Todo -> In Progress`)\nAttach an `agent_interceptor` or Tier A Wasm `sync_validator` on `Todo -> In Progress` to verify that work items touching authentication, RBAC, or database migrations include a threat model checklist and zero pasted credentials before engineering begins.\n\n### Idea 3: Automated Definition-of-Done QA Gate (`In Progress -> Done`)\nRequire completed acceptance criteria and documented unit/QA verification evidence before any card can land in `Done`:\n- **Positive Transition (KAN-1 Approved):** A ticket with checked Acceptance Criteria and documented Verification Proof passes the gate, lands in `Done`, and receives a persisted `## \u2705\ud83e\udd16 Workflow Transition Gate Approved` rationale comment.\n\n![Positive Gate Approval Landing Ticket in Done](images/66-qa-gate-positive-approved-done.png)\n\n- **Negative Transition (KAN-2 Vetoed):** An unverified or impossible ticket (such as `\"Build a rocket ship and serve ice cream on Saturn\"` with no design doc, no tests, and no proof) is **vetoed** and kept safely in `In Progress` with an itemized `\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16 Gate` rejection comment.\n\n![Negative Gate Veto Blocking Saturn Ice Cream Ticket in In Progress](images/67-qa-gate-negative-saturn-ice-cream-vetoed.png)\n\n---\n\n## Live Walkthrough: Reality-Checker Done Gate with Self-Hosted Ollama (`nemotron-3-super:latest`)\n\nThis walkthrough captures an end-to-end verification on a fresh standalone `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` container backed by a self-hosted **Ollama** server running `nemotron-3-super:latest` (123.6B Q4_K_M reasoning model).\n\n### 1. Register and Probe the Self-Hosted Ollama Provider (`/admin/ai`)\n1. Open **AI Providers & Token Caps Studio** (`/admin/ai`) and register an `ollama` provider pointing to your LAN Ollama host (for example `http://192.168.1.101:11434`).\n2. Click **Probe Models** to verify live connectivity and allowlist `nemotron-3-super:latest`.\n3. Set the provider cutoff timeout to at least `60000` ms for large reasoning models (`nemotron-3-super:latest` typically completes a full gate evaluation in `13s` to `25s`).\n\n![Ollama Provider Probed with nemotron-3-super:latest](images/rc-ollama-01-provider-probe.png)\n\n### 2. Define the `reality-checker` Agent (`/admin/agents`)\n1. Open **Autonomous Agent Definitions Studio** (`/admin/agents`) and click **+ Register Agent**.\n2. Configure Harness Kind as `in_app_sdk`, Provider Kind as `ollama`, and Model as `nemotron-3-super:latest`.\n3. Set Timeout to `60000` ms (matching the `StructuredRequest.ttft_deadline_ms` limit of `<= 300000` ms, 1..300000).\n4. Enter System Instructions defaulting to `\"reject\"` unless the ticket includes checked-off Definition of Done items, passing unit test and build outputs, and explicit forensic verification proof.\n\n![Reality-Checker Agent Definition using ollama/nemotron-3-super:latest](images/rc-ollama-02-agent-definition.png)\n\n### 3. Attach the Fail-Closed Hook on `In Progress -> Done`\n1. In your project's **Workflow Transition Studio** (`/projects/<KEY>/settings/workflows`), attach an `agent_interceptor` hook named `reality-checker-done-gate` targeting `In Progress -> Done` with **Failure Policy: Fail-Closed** and agent `@reality-checker`.\n\n![Reality-Checker Workflow Hook Attached to In Progress to Done](images/rc-ollama-03-workflow-hook.png)\n\n### 4. Positive Transition (`RCD-1`: Approved with Comment and Allowed to `Done`)\nA work item (`RCD-1: Implement /healthz Readiness Endpoint with Unit Tests`) containing checked-off Definition of Done items, passing `pytest` / `ruff` / `mypy` results, and documented `docs/qa/RCD-1/` proof artifacts is moved from `In Progress` to `Done`:\n- The API returns **HTTP 202 Accepted** and dispatches the asynchronous gate to `in_app_sdk (ollama/nemotron-3-super:latest)`.\n- Within ~13 seconds, the gate posts a persisted **## \u2705\ud83e\udd16 Workflow Transition Gate Approved** comment (`All Definition of Done items are completed and forensic verification passes.`) and commits the state transition to **Done**.\n\n![Positive Transition Board View Landing RCD-1 in Done](images/rc-ollama-04-positive-done.png)\n\n![Positive Transition Ticket Activity and Approval Comment on RCD-1](images/rc-ollama-05-positive-comment.png)\n\n### 5. Negative Transition (`RCD-2`: Vetoed with Comment and Blocked in `In Progress`)\nA work item (`RCD-2: Deploy Quantum Warp Drive to Production`) with unchecked Definition of Done items, missing tests, and no verification proof is moved from `In Progress` to `Done`:\n- Within ~22 seconds, `nemotron-3-super:latest` evaluates the spotlighted ticket context and returns a `reject` verdict.\n- Because the hook is **Fail-Closed**, the transition is **blocked** (`RCD-2` remains in **In Progress**) and `@realitycheckerqagate` persists a **\ud83d\uded1\ud83e\udd16 Gate** rejection comment citing the exact missing criteria (skipped design document, zero unit tests, unrun build/lint checks, and lack of trusted proof).\n\n![Negative Transition Board View Keeping RCD-2 Blocked in In Progress](images/rc-ollama-06-negative-blocked.png)\n\n![Negative Transition Ticket Activity and Rejection Comment on RCD-2](images/rc-ollama-07-negative-comment.png)\n\n"
